    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "<|channel>thought\n<channel|>That's an interesting mix of suggestions. I'm curious about the \"community cohesion\" part though. If we're looking at arrest rates or crime data as a proxy for cohesion, isn't there a high risk of just penalizing neighborhoods that are more diverse or have higher turnover? \n\nSpecifically, if a neighborhood has a lot of transient populations or a high density of minority groups, how do we ensure the model isn't just interpreting \"diversity\" or \"cultural density\" as \"volatility\"? I want to make sure we aren't accidentally building a model that just flags certain demographics as high risk through these proxies. Should we be looking for specific indicators that distinguish between a neighborhood that is \"unstable\" vs one that is just \"culturally different\"?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
